The i18n defaults `addFilter` and `addFilterTitle` are now `''`, and `StatsFilter` passes `addButtonText={filters.length ? '' : ''}`. The button falls back to `mergedI18n.addFilter`, which is also empty. That leaves an icon-only `<button>` with no text, no `title`, and no `aria-label`. Screen readers will announce it as an unnamed button, and sighted users lose the hover hint too.

The ternary is also dead. Both branches return `''`, so the "Add filter" / "Filter" distinction is gone. Shipping this as the default for every `Filters` consumer changes the shared component, not just Stats. The `addFilter` default is shown as the button label whenever a consumer passes no `addButtonText`.

::diff{file="apps/stats/src/views/Stats/components/stats-filter.tsx" lines="494-500"}

::callout{type="warning"}
If the icon-only design is intended, keep the visible text out but give the button an accessible name, for example `aria-label` from a non-empty i18n string, and keep `title` populated.

::suggestion{file="apps/stats/src/views/Stats/components/stats-filter.tsx" lines="497-498"}
addButtonIcon={<LucideIcon.FunnelPlus />}
addButtonText={undefined}

Also restore a non-empty `addFilterTitle`/`addFilter` default in `filters.tsx` or add `aria-label` in the `Filters` button.

The audience field now sets `defaultOperator: 'is any of'`. That string is the human label for `is_any_of`, the key that `createOperatorsFromI18n` defines for multiselect (filters.tsx ~line 800). `addFilterWithOption` writes `field.defaultOperator` straight into the new filter, so audience filters will be saved with `operator: 'is any of'`. Every other multiselect uses `is_any_of`.

The display still looks correct, because `FilterOperatorDropdown` falls back to `formatOperator` when `operators.find` misses. Any logic that compares the stored operator to `'is_any_of'`, such as NQL serialization, operator-specific chip edits, or URL persistence, will treat audience differently from the other multiselects.

::diff{file="apps/stats/src/views/Stats/components/stats-filter.tsx" lines="444-458"}

::suggestion{file="apps/stats/src/views/Stats/components/stats-filter.tsx" lines="453-453"}
                        defaultOperator: 'is_any_of',

The PR adds `data-[state=closed]:!animation-none data-[state=closed]:!duration-0` to three `PopoverContent` elements. Tailwind has no `animation-none` utility. The utility is `animate-none`. The `PopoverContent` in `apps/shade/src/components/ui/popover.tsx` (line 22) closes with `data-[state=closed]:animate-out`, so the override never applies and the close animation still plays.

`!duration-0` also doesn't help. In Tailwind, `duration-*` sets `transition-duration`. The shade popover animates with `tailwindcss-animate`'s `animation` shorthand, and that is controlled by `animation-duration`, not `transition-duration`. So the intended "no close animation" effect isn't produced by either class. The result is dead CSS that reads as if it works.

::diff{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="2078-2084"}

::suggestion{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="2081-2081"}
                                'p-0 data-[state=closed]:!animate-none',

`autoCloseOnSelect` is implemented in two places that do different things. The inline path (line 1136) calls `onClose?.()` directly. The non-inline path (line 1273) calls `handleClose()`, which also clears `searchInput`. The inline path's `onClose` is provided by `Filters` and also resets state, so the practical behaviour is fine. The two branches should still share one close helper so a future change to `handleClose` reaches both.

Relatedly, the inline close never clears `tempSelectedValues` in `Filters`. The next field pick reseeds it, so this is harmless today. The coupling is subtle, though, and a reader has to trace `setTempSelectedValues` to see that.

::diff{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="1131-1138"}

::suggestion{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="1135-1137"}
                                                    if (field.autoCloseOnSelect) {
                                                        handleClose();
                                                    }

The multiselect branch changed `const newValues` to `var newValues`. Nothing depends on function-scoped hoisting here, and the rest of the file uses `const`. This looks like an accidental edit. It is not a runtime bug, but it is an easy style fix.

::diff{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="1123-1127"}

::suggestion{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="1125-1125"}
                                                    const newValues = [...effectiveValues, option.value] as T[];

The `Filters` popover width now resolves as `selectedFieldForOptions?.className || popoverContentClassName || 'w-[200px]'`. Before, the caller's `popoverContentClassName` was always merged in. Now any field that sets `className` silently discards the `popoverContentClassName` prop passed to `Filters`. The post field works around this by setting both `className: 'w-80'` and `popoverContentClassName: 'w-80'`, which is redundant and shows the precedence is easy to trip over.

Same story for `SelectOptionsPopover` at line ~1204: `field.className || 'w-[200px]'` replaces the old merge. This is probably intended, but it is a behaviour change for any consumer that relied on merging.

::diff{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="2078-2086"}

Rather than special-casing audience inside shade, the PR adds `autoCloseOnSelect` to `FilterFieldConfig` with a comment. Consumers opt in per field, the default multiselect behaviour is unchanged, and `selectedOptionsClassName` is reused for the hidden chips instead of adding a new prop. This keeps the shared component generic.

One trade-off to decide on: with auto-close on a multiselect, a user can pick only one audience per open. That is probably what the PR wants, but it is a UX choice worth stating in the PR description.

::diff{file="apps/shade/src/components/ui/filters.tsx" lines="747-752"}
